WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.900
(Journalist) The parish priest of Pasai Donibane, José Ramón — good morning. I was telling him about you, and he said I could call you "tú".

00:12.200 --> 00:14.600
Of course — it's closer, more friendly.

00:15.200 --> 00:20.300
(Journalist) So, José Ramón, what is the parish priest of Pasai Donibane doing this Tuesday?

00:21.400 --> 00:26.300
(Journalist) Dressed for the mountains, with his walking poles — he is coming back from a hike.

00:26.300 --> 00:31.100
That's it. I really like hiking, and nature is a privileged place.

00:31.400 --> 00:34.800
And here, needless to say, we have the sea and the mountains, so it's a joy.

00:35.300 --> 00:39.600
I got up early, made the most of the morning, and joined two pilgrims on the Camino de Santiago.

00:40.000 --> 00:42.800
So I'm showing them things, chatting and sharing.

00:43.100 --> 00:46.000
(Journalist) Many people may not know the Camino de Santiago also passes through here.

00:46.200 --> 00:50.000
That's right — the Camino del Norte. The most travelled one is the Camino Francés,

00:50.000 --> 00:55.300
but this is the Camino del Norte: a bit more of a leg-breaker, but I think more varied, more rural,

00:55.900 --> 00:58.700
and a very rewarding experience, without a doubt.

00:59.300 --> 01:05.400
(Journalist) I'm curious: how does the parish priest of Pasai Donibane live the town's fiestas?

01:05.500 --> 01:06.700
Flat out, with the people.

01:07.300 --> 01:12.200
Just yesterday, San Pantaleón, the day of the fishermen, there was a community meal,

01:12.900 --> 01:18.900
with people enjoying being neighbours, the music, everything.

01:19.400 --> 01:21.400
(Journalist) How many masses do you celebrate during the fiestas?

01:22.000 --> 01:26.200
Well, on Santa Ana, which fell on a Sunday, three in a row.

01:26.500 --> 01:28.000
But that's an exception.

01:28.600 --> 01:31.900
Yesterday one Eucharist, one mass, for San Pantaleón.

01:32.700 --> 01:38.500
(Journalist) Do we invite people to come or not? Sometimes there is this debate: not too many people, because we are fine as we are —

01:38.700 --> 01:42.100
(Journalist) — but on the other hand, come and visit us. Where do you stand?

01:42.400 --> 01:45.800
I think it is something to experience. San Juan wins your heart.

01:46.300 --> 01:54.200
So I would invite people to take a little stroll and enjoy this very special village,

01:54.400 --> 01:58.300
with its very, very particular architecture,

01:58.600 --> 02:01.500
and to enjoy the people too, who are very welcoming.

02:01.900 --> 02:05.200
San Juan is an open and welcoming village.

02:05.300 --> 02:09.600
(Journalist) And for the rest of the fiestas, José Ramón, what shouldn't we miss?

02:09.900 --> 02:14.100
The day of San Ignacio — it is the finale, the culmination of the fiestas.

02:14.700 --> 02:19.800
San Ignacio is the patron saint of Gipuzkoa, and here it is celebrated very intensely with the bateleras,

02:20.400 --> 02:24.000
a re-enactment of the women who rowed the boats across the bay.

02:24.300 --> 02:29.300
So I think the bateleras are worth it — enjoy them at midday, go and see them.

02:29.900 --> 02:36.700
(Journalist) Today without his habit, in his hiking clothes: the parish priest of Pasai Donibane kindly spoke to us. Thank you.
